<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!--
  Windows Task Scheduler XML for the Claude MCP container stack.

  v0.2.10 (Bug L2 — cross-OS boot-service materialization): on Windows
  the systemd unit / launchd plist concepts map to a Scheduled Task
  registered against the user's logon trigger. install.py renders this
  template and imports it via `schtasks /Create /XML <file> /F`. Runs
  per-user without admin elevation.

  v0.2.14 (Bug #2 — Windows PowerShell wrapper): the previous version of
  this template invoked `bash scripts/launch-claude-mcp-stack.sh` via
  cmd.exe, which required Git Bash or WSL on PATH. install.py logged a
  soft warning when neither was present, but the Scheduled Task itself
  then failed silently on every logon. Since v0.2.14 the sibling PS1
  wrapper `scripts/launch-claude-mcp-stack.ps1` ships alongside the
  bash one, and this template invokes it via powershell.exe — which is
  guaranteed to be present on every Windows 10+ install (Windows
  PowerShell 5.1) without any third-party dependency.

  Why this exists:
    - The wrapper script scripts/launch-claude-mcp-stack.ps1 is a
      PowerShell 5.1+ port of the bash sibling. It carries the same
      runtime detection (VCT_CONTAINER_RUNTIME env preference -> runtime.txt
      -> auto-probe), daemon-access validation (`docker info` Server
      section / `podman info` exit 0), compose override resolution
      (compose.override.yaml + VCT_STACK_COMPOSE_OVERRIDE), and soft-fail
      discipline as the .sh wrapper.
    - StartWhenAvailable + per-user logon trigger matches the Linux
      `systemctl user enable` + `loginctl enable-linger` pair: the
      task runs whenever the user logs in, no admin scope required.
      (Note: the actual systemctl invocation uses the long flag
      `systemctl __user enable` — written this way in the comment to
      avoid the double-hyphen sequence that XML 1.0 forbids inside
      comments.)

  GPU note (Windows):
    Docker Desktop on Windows uses the WSL2 backend; GPU passthrough
    requires the NVIDIA-WSL2 driver (host-side, separate install) AND
    Docker Desktop ≥ 4.16. podman on Windows runs via podman-machine
    (also WSL2-backed); same WSL2 GPU semantics apply. The PS1 wrapper's
    Test-HasNvidia probes the Windows host's nvidia-smi if present, but
    CDI files (/var/run/cdi/nvidia.yaml) live inside the WSL2 distro
    and are not pollable from the host without `wsl.exe -d` indirection.
    The current Windows policy: if nvidia-smi is on the host PATH and
    reports a GPU, gpu_mode=gpu; otherwise CPU-only. The container
    runtime (Docker Desktop / podman-machine) handles WSL CDI internally.

  Template substitutions (filled in by install.py):
    {{LABEL}}              — task name (no path separators)
    {{WORKING_DIR}}        — directory containing compose.yaml
    {{WRAPPER_SCRIPT}}     — absolute path to launch-claude-mcp-stack.ps1
                             (v0.2.14: was .sh; powershell.exe accepts
                             both forward and backslash separators)
    {{LOG_FILE}}           — absolute path to the unit's log file
    {{CREATED_AT}}         — ISO8601 timestamp at materialization time
    {{USER_ID}}            — Windows user ID (e.g. DOMAIN\\user) for the
                             logon trigger context

  Environment propagation (PR-32, 2026-05-16; revised v0.2.14):
    The PS1 wrapper reads three env vars set by the Scheduled Task:

        VCT_STACK_WORKING_DIR    — directory containing compose.yaml
        VCT_STACK_LOG_FILE       — absolute path to the wrapper's log file
        VCT_STACK_COMPOSE_OVERRIDE — overlay compose file (defaults to
                                     `compose.override.yaml`)

    Prior to PR-32 this Windows scheduled task fired bash with NONE of
    them set, so the wrapper silently fell through to its defaults
    (which on Windows resolve to paths that may not exist for the
    current user, breaking compose-up). Mirrors the systemd template's
    `Environment=VCT_STACK_*=...` lines.

    v0.2.14 mechanism: we wrap powershell.exe in `cmd.exe /c "set
    VAR=val && set VAR2=val2 && set VAR3=val3 && powershell.exe
    -ExecutionPolicy Bypass -File <script>"`. cmd.exe is always on PATH
    on Windows; the `set` builtin is per-process scope (it does not leak
    into the user's interactive environment); the `&&` chain ensures
    powershell.exe inherits the variables. -ExecutionPolicy Bypass keeps
    the script runnable even on machines that ship with the default
    Restricted policy (no need to elevate or modify the policy
    permanently). -NoProfile skips the user's PowerShell profile
    (faster boot, no profile-script side effects).
-->
<Task version="1.4" xmlns="http://schemas.microsoft.com/windows/2004/02/mit/task">
  <RegistrationInfo>
    <Date>{{CREATED_AT}}</Date>
    <Author>{{USER_ID}}</Author>
    <Description>Claude MCP container stack (weaviate, ollama, code_embed) — brings up at user logon via launch-claude-mcp-stack.ps1.</Description>
    <URI>\{{LABEL}}</URI>
  </RegistrationInfo>
  <Triggers>
    <LogonTrigger>
      <Enabled>true</Enabled>
      <UserId>{{USER_ID}}</UserId>
    </LogonTrigger>
  </Triggers>
  <Principals>
    <Principal id="Author">
      <UserId>{{USER_ID}}</UserId>
      <LogonType>InteractiveToken</LogonType>
      <RunLevel>LeastPrivilege</RunLevel>
    </Principal>
  </Principals>
  <Settings>
    <MultipleInstancesPolicy>IgnoreNew</MultipleInstancesPolicy>
    <DisallowStartIfOnBatteries>false</DisallowStartIfOnBatteries>
    <StopIfGoingOnBatteries>false</StopIfGoingOnBatteries>
    <AllowHardTerminate>true</AllowHardTerminate>
    <StartWhenAvailable>true</StartWhenAvailable>
    <RunOnlyIfNetworkAvailable>false</RunOnlyIfNetworkAvailable>
    <IdleSettings>
      <StopOnIdleEnd>false</StopOnIdleEnd>
      <RestartOnIdle>false</RestartOnIdle>
    </IdleSettings>
    <AllowStartOnDemand>true</AllowStartOnDemand>
    <Enabled>true</Enabled>
    <Hidden>false</Hidden>
    <RunOnlyIfIdle>false</RunOnlyIfIdle>
    <WakeToRun>false</WakeToRun>
    <ExecutionTimeLimit>PT0S</ExecutionTimeLimit>
    <Priority>7</Priority>
  </Settings>
  <!--
    Action invocation note (PR-22 + PR-32, 2026-05-16; revised v0.2.14
    Bug #2 to invoke the PS1 sibling):

    Task Scheduler XML has no native env-var declarations in <Actions>.
    We propagate the three VCT_STACK_* env vars by wrapping powershell.exe
    in `cmd.exe /c "set ...&& set ...&& powershell.exe -File WRAPPER"`.
    cmd.exe is chosen because it is always present on Windows PATH and
    is the simplest way to chain `set` commands before the final exec.

    powershell.exe (the in-box Windows PowerShell 5.1 host at
    %SystemRoot%\System32\WindowsPowerShell\v1.0\powershell.exe) is also
    always present — every Windows 10+ install ships with it. No Git
    Bash / WSL dependency. The PS1 wrapper is compatible with both
    powershell.exe (5.1) and pwsh.exe (Core 7+); we pick powershell.exe
    for maximum out-of-box reach.

    Env vars set:
      * VCT_STACK_WORKING_DIR={{WORKING_DIR}}        — wrapper working dir
      * VCT_STACK_LOG_FILE={{LOG_FILE}}              — wrapper log target
      * VCT_STACK_COMPOSE_OVERRIDE=compose.override.yaml — user-machine
        override (see scripts/launch-claude-mcp-stack.ps1) that lets the
        launcher's Storage UX override (PR-10A) take effect at boot.
        Default value matches podman-compose's auto-load convention;
        without it the wrapper's explicit `-f compose.yaml` bypasses
        auto-load.

    Mirrors the systemd unit's three `Environment=` lines (see
    templates/systemd/claude-mcp-containers.service.template).

    Argument breakdown for powershell.exe:
      -NoProfile          Skip the user's PowerShell profile (faster
                          boot, no profile-script side effects).
      -ExecutionPolicy Bypass
                          Per-invocation policy override; does NOT
                          modify the user's persistent policy. Allows
                          unsigned scripts (our PS1 is not signed).
      -File "WRAPPER"     Execute the script file (vs -Command).
                          Quoting survives the cmd.exe -> powershell.exe
                          boundary via `&quot;`.

    The `&amp;&amp;` chain XML-escapes `&&` so cmd.exe inherits the env
    var across the chained `set` commands. `&quot;` escapes the inner
    double-quote around {{WRAPPER_SCRIPT}} so the wrapper path is
    correctly quoted when powershell.exe receives it.
  -->
  <Actions Context="Author">
    <Exec>
      <Command>cmd.exe</Command>
      <Arguments>/c "set VCT_STACK_WORKING_DIR={{WORKING_DIR}}&amp;&amp; set VCT_STACK_LOG_FILE={{LOG_FILE}}&amp;&amp; set VCT_STACK_COMPOSE_OVERRIDE=compose.override.yaml&amp;&amp; powershell.exe -NoProfile -ExecutionPolicy Bypass -File &quot;{{WRAPPER_SCRIPT}}&quot;"</Arguments>
      <WorkingDirectory>{{WORKING_DIR}}</WorkingDirectory>
    </Exec>
  </Actions>
</Task>
